  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disrupting the Illegal Wildlife Trade in Grey Parrots (Psittacus erithacus) in Cameroon, Central Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">African grey parrots are intensively hunted throughout their range for the trade in live birds and parrot parts. Since the 1990s, much of the legal trade has shifted from West Africa to Central Africa where local populations are rapidly declining. African grey parrots were uplisted to Appendix I at the 17th CITES Conference of the Parties (CoP) in 2016. This uplisting bans the commercial trade and harvest of wild-caught parrots. In Cameroon, the historic range of these birds has been reduced by more than half. To address the illegal trapping and killing of wild parrots for the live bird and parts trade, this two-year grant will support: training of law enforcement personnel based at ports of entry in Cameroon to better detect and intercept parrot trafficking; training of field-based law enforcement personnel working in and around key sites for parrot poaching and trafficking; mentoring and support for trained law enforcement agents; training of park authority personnel in management of intelligence information in order to conduct intelligence-led field operations to interdict parrot trafficking; market surveys to gather information about the illegal parrot trade; and identification of key sites, including major roosts and forest clearings, to better direct site-based protection activities.</narrative>
  </description>
